SM(585,{"n":"Titus 1-3","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Titus 1-3.mp3","d":3218.2,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study holy scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[22.56,"So I'm gonna go ahead and get started, and when we get started in just a moment, uh tonight we're gonna be covering two whole books because they're both very short."],[33.52,"Um those are uh St."],[36.0,"Paul's Epistle to Titus and St."],[38.96,"Paul's Epistle to Philemon, uh the latter of which is like a paragraph."],[44.48,"So um that's why we're gonna be talking about both of them uh this evening."],[50.72,"Um so uh I'm going to basically, and I anticipate ancient Fitha will probably snip this in half. and release it as two separate ones"],[62.36,"Um so uh I'll sort of introduce each separately to facilitate them doing that."],[67.72,"I could be I could be a jerk and just do both introductions now and then go through both books and the and make make uh poor poor Tim Wenger."],[79.32,"Shout out to poor Tim Wenger who edits this."],[82.04,"Because I do nothing to edit it and nothing to make his job any easier at all ever."],[87.24,"Uh but um yeah, I won't do that."],[92.44,"I'll be nice."],[93.24,"So uh the introduction to St."],[95.24,"Paul's Epistle to Titus is uh gonna be pretty short because we're basically dittoing a lot of the stuff we said about 1 and second Timothy."],[104.84,"And in fact, as we go through his epistle to St"],[110.48,"Titus, we'll see that he's even going to be saying a lot of the same things that he said, particularly in 1 Timothy."],[121.6,"And this is it was written around the same time, so St."],[125.76,"Paul at this point is in his second Roman imprisonment."],[130.88,"He knows that he's coming to the end of his life, as we've seen, going through 1st and 2 Timothy."],[138.48,"And he's now, these these are called the pastoral epistles, these three, 1st and 2nd Timothy and Titus, because he has this sort of mentorship role with both St."],[148.64,"Timothy and St."],[149.52,"Titus. And these letters come out of that as he's preparing them for a sort of expanded ministry."],[158.52,"The office that we now call bishop, that he refers to as the episcopacy in 1 Timothy."],[167.4,"And he's preparing them for that, knowing that he is shortly going to no longer be with them in the flesh, and other apostles likewise."],[177.88,"So we're now transitioning into transitioning into another era of the church."],[182.2,"And we talked about, I'll just say it now for when we get to it."],[187.32,"We talked about how there's this idea that, well, because these letters talk about these offices of the church of Episcopals and Resbyteros, Presbyter and Deacon, that this comes from some later period of the church."],[205.6,"But in actuality, when you understand that transition, then you understand why, for example, while the apostles are still alive, there's not a lot of difference between a presbyter and an episcopal."],[216.26,"Between a bishop and a what we'd now call a priest."],[220.34,"Because the apostles are still there."],[222.36,"But then once the apostles are gone, these become more formal, more formalized because the bishops are seen as those who have been designated by the apostles to carry on after them."],[236.6,"St. Timothy and St."],[237.72,"Titus being two of those."],[239.08,"So we've read the two uh letters that we still have that St"],[245.92,"Paul sent to St."],[246.88,"Timothy. Um Saint Titus is not as prominent as we've seen."],[252.48,"We're coming to the end of reading St."],[254.08,"Paul's epistles. So we saw St."],[256.24,"Timothy come up over and over again."],[258.08,"St. Timothy was listed as the co-writer of some of those epistles."],[262.0,"Um and we haven't seen that with Saint Titus."],[265.44,"But we know we've seen that there was this group of people Saint Luke, Saint Mark, um Saint uh Silas, uh St."],[275.65,"Barnabas, sort of around Saint Paul as he's traveled."],[279.81,"And then there have been other people from churches who have joined the Christian. community who have sort of become important members of the Apostolic Church"],[288.65,"They're usually numbered among the 70, like Saint Sprisilla and Aquila, you know, whose names come up again and again."],[294.25,"Uh and so Saint Titus is one of these people."],[297.05,"And so um the letter to St."],[300.17,"Titus is going to be shorter because, as we've gleaned from reading St."],[304.81,"Paul's epistles, there even though St."],[307.29,"Paul does have this sort of mentorship role with Saint Titus, it's not quite as close as his relationship with St."],[314.09,"Timothy. Um, and then so that will explain some of the differences, and then the reason for the similarities should be obvious."],[323.77,"He's preparing Saint Titus for a similar uh type of uh ministry."],[328.81,"Um so that said, uh, unless anybody has any Yes, sir"],[335.25,"Do we know where Timothy and Titus were bishops?"],[340.69,"Well, Saint Timothy went out from Ephesus and was actually at several places."],[347.73,"As we're gonna see, Saint Titus Titus is in Crete."],[354.61,"Um and we're gonna see that's gonna be that's gonna come up right in the in the letter that he's particularly sort of assigning Saint Titus to Crete."],[367.41,"And Titus, of course, is quite obviously a Roman name."],[371.33,"Yes. So unless there's any other questions or comments or things left unsaid, uh we'll go ahead and start in Saint Paul's epistle to Saint Titus chapter one, verse one."],[390.42,"Paul, a bond servant of God and an apostle of Jesus Christ, according to the faith of God's elect and the acknowledgement of the truth, which accords with godliness."],[405.62,"So this is Saint Paul's usual introduction, but now as we've seen in all of his epistles, uh, even though this is very short, he's seeding in already some of his themes."],[417.3,"So bond servant, of course, means slave"],[421.59,"So he's saying that he's a slave of God and an apostle once sent by Jesus Christ."],[429.27,"According to the, as we've said, faithfulness is better, or faith is better translated faithfulness, according to the faithfulness of God's elect and the acknowledgement of the truth, which accords with godliness."],[443.27,"So St. Paul is not is covering both ends."],[448.15,"So on one hand, he's a servant of God, and he's been sent by Christ, and on the other hand, the church."],[456.81,"The Christian communities that he's visited."],[459.15,"And particularly the godly among them. give testimony to the reality of St"],[465.59,"Paul's ministry and his calling, and that he's a servant of God."],[470.32,"So he's received that calling, and that people can attest, from the human side and from God's side."],[482.02,"Well, it's the same thing as we saw in 1st and 2 Timothy."],[486.39,"The elect are just the remnant, the faithful."],[495.88,"Uh verse 2: In hope of eternal life, which God who cannot lie promised before time began, but has in due time manifested his word through preaching, which was committed to me according to the commandment of God our Savior."],[517.32,"So verse 2."],[520.86,"In hope of eternal life."],[522.06,"So that's the goal."],[524.94,"And he says that God promised it before time began."],[527.98,"Well, what does that mean?"],[528.94,"Who did he promise it to the point being St."],[533.82,"Paul's saying this was God's purpose all along."],[537.1,"This is the purpose for which he created humanity was to share his life with them eternally."],[544.87,"So this is not just some new thing."],[548.41,"Salvation is not a response to like Adam and Eve sin, like God had a plan A, and then they sinned."],[555.05,"And so he said, Okay, well, plan B."],[558.39,"This is this is God's plan from the from the very beginning."],[562.39,"And because he can't lie, the idea is that therefore it's sure."],[566.65,"And it's been sure the whole time."],[569.27,"That this was going to come to fruition."],[572.87,"So this was promised all the way back then."],[577.12,"It's always been true."],[578.56,"It's been manifested."],[580.34,"We've now seen it."],[582.04,"We've seen St."],[582.84,"Paul does this a lot."],[583.8,"So there are things that are eternal and eternally true, and then at some point they enter into human experience."],[589.56,"experience We them, we find out about them."],[590.04,"So another good example of this that we saw was when St"],[597.12,"Paul referred to Christ as the lamb slain before the foundation of the world."],[601.68,"And you're like, well, wait a minute."],[603.2,"I thought he was slain in, you know, 30 AD."],[611.36,"Both are true for St."],[613.44,"Paul. So Christ sacrifices an eternal reality."],[619.14,"But that eternal reality enters into human experience on a particular day at a particular time."],[627.27,"Enters into our history."],[629.19,"And so this is the same kind of idea he's talking about here."],[632.63,"So eternal life is always God's purpose."],[635.96,"The eternal life of the faithful, this is why he created humanity."],[639.88,"But this has now been this plan."],[642.46,"This mystery, has now been revealed in these latter days."],[652.4,"And uh let me suggest that word there should be capitalized."],[661.14,"He hasn't manifested, like, when we see word and not capitalized, people tend to think of the Bible."],[667.83,"Or something. He's not saying God has manifested the Bible through preaching."],[672.63,"That that doesn't make any sense."],[675.29,"Because his preaching just started recently, and the whole Old Testament, what he calls the scriptures, was written centuries before that."],[685.97,"So when he says he's manifested his word, it should be word capital W, talking about Christ, because Christ is the way in which that plan, that mystery is manifest."],[698.07,"And Christ is manifest to Saint Titus and the people in the Gentile communities that St."],[704.23,"Paul went to, how is Christ made manifest to them?"],[708.41,"The Judeans saw him in person, but they see him through St."],[713.77,"Paul's preaching, through him coming and preaching the gospel to them."],[716.81,"That's how Christ is first made manifest to them."],[723.49,"And that's why it's particularly his preaching of the gospel, because that language St."],[727.97,"Paul uses at the end of verse 3, which was committed to me according to the commandment of God our Savior."],[734.26,"That's what was committed to him was the gospel."],[737.14,"Christ committed the gospel to him."],[738.74,"He went and preached it, and that's how Christ was then manifest to them."],[745.7,"So he's already set up some of the themes now that he's going to talk to St."],[749.62,"Titus about as he's sort of commissioning him."],[752.9,"Verse 4, to Titus, a true son in our common faith."],[756.9,"Notice the language is a little different because his relationship with Saint Titus is a little different."],[761.7,"Over and over again with St."],[762.82,"Timothy, he referred to my son."],[764.26,"son. That they had this very direct father-son relationship, whereas Saint Titus is more a of the church"],[771.68,"He's a son of the, you know, so uh Saint Paul does have a sort of fatherly or patronal relationship with him, but it's less direct and intimate."],[786.96,"Grace, mercy, and peace from God the Father and the Lord Jesus Christ, our Savior."],[791.52,"That's fairly typical introduction for Saint Paul."],[797.04,"Verse 5. For this reason I left you in Crete, that you should set in order the things that are lacking, and appoint elders in every city as I commanded you."],[807.6,"you He says, This is why I left in Crete."],[808.56,"Paul had been there"],[809.56,"So he gets right to it, as I said."],[814.36,"St. He told St."],[817.4,"Titus to stay."],[818.52,"So he's not sending them there to do this."],[820.44,"He left them there to do this."],[822.36,"And that's to pull the communities into good order."],[826.41,"Now, first of all, you could see why the argument that this must have come for some late stage in the church."],[836.01,"This has to be like a hundred years later."],[838.25,"Like, why would you have to wait decades and decades?"],[842.81,"Why would you have to wait like three generations for people in a group to like organize and have a leader?"],[853.45,"Like, that makes no sense"],[858.89,"Like, you don't plan a church and then wait a hundred years to ask for a priest."],[865.58,"Like, that makes no sense at all."],[868.83,"So it's very obvious that as soon as you have any kind of sizable community, you would want to establish some kind of order and structure."],[882.24,"You don't have to wait."],[884.0,"So it this would seem to imply the exact opposite."],[887.92,"This would seem to imply that the gospel has sort of just been preached by St."],[893.52,"Paul in Crete."],[894.96,"Paul wants St"],[896.12,"They have now these communities of Christians in the different cities, and St."],[902.28,"Titus to sort of get them organized."],[905.18,"And appoint elders."],[906.94,"And we talked about how presbyter means elder."],[911.02,"We talked about how this is just not a new thing they made up."],[913.98,"Like, hey, how should we structure these?"],[915.66,"Well, let's have."],[916.94,"But this is coming from the Old Testament."],[919.36,"There were the elders of the tribes of Israel."],[921.76,"The same word is used in the Greek Old Testament, presbyteros."],[926.97,"That's what the who the elders are."],[928.73,"Every tribe had them, every clan had them."],[931.69,"And so they're structuring the Christian communities in the same way that the Old Testament community was structured."],[939.45,"Let's start this new, we're starting this new religion"],[939.89,"Because they see it as a continuation."],[942.31,"This is not, well, we're done with that now."],[946.63,"How should we do it?"],[947.73,"It's this idea, it's a continuation."],[950.29,"And so he says to St."],[951.89,"Titus, you need to go and you need to appoint elders in each city."],[957.01,"In each of these communities, you have elders, who are going to govern it."],[962.07,"And if Saint Titus is the one who goes and appoints and ordains the elders, the presbyters, the priests, what is he?"],[972.09,"He's their supervisor."],[973.74,"Overseer. He's the episcopos."],[975.86,"He's the bishop."],[977.94,"And again, you don't need a hundred years for that to develop."],[981.44,"For an apostle to send someone to appoint priests at all the cities and to oversee all the whole thing."],[987.64,"Does it require more than six or nine months."],[990.3,"After having uh preached there."],[995.9,"Verse 6. If a man is blameless, the husband of one wife, having faithful children, not accused of dissipation or insubordination, for a bishop, an overseer must be blameless as a steward of God, not self-willed, not quick-tempered, not given to wine, not violent, not greedy for money, but hospitable, a lover of what is good, sober-minded, just holy, self-controlled, holding fast the faithful word as he has been taught. that he may be able by sound doctrine both to exhort and convict those who contradict"],[1032.74,"So we see this is very similar to what we saw in 1 Timothy."],[1036.02,"It's not quite verbatim, it's condensed a little bit."],[1038.66,"But the St."],[1039.7,"Paul doesn't change his requirements for ordained leaders, depending on where you are in the world."],[1047.22,"So we've that's that's sort of the typical the typical thing we've seen."],[1054.42,"Verse 10. For there are many insubordinate, both idle talkers and deceivers, especially those of the circumcision, whose mouths must be stopped, who subvert whole households, teaching things which they ought not for the sake of dishonest gains."],[1071.02,"Okay. So we'll pause there."],[1073.1,"So he's he says it's really important that we have clear leadership."],[1079.07,"Coming down from an apostle."],[1081.81,"Why is it important that we have clear leadership directly connected to an apostle?"],[1087.01,"Well, because there's a whole bunch of people who want to show up and want to teach things and start movements and be leaders, in these newborn communities."],[1096.17,"This is another thing that suggests a long time hasn't passed."],[1099.61,"Because if a long time had passed, some kind of leadership would have evolved naturally."],[1104.49,"And the discussion would be different."],[1106.59,"But when you have this nascent group that's just getting together, has just heard the gospel, doesn't know a lot of doctrine, doesn't know a lot."],[1113.75,"Um, this is when people turn up."],[1117.41,"And want to draw followings after themselves."],[1120.61,"And so he says there's a whole bunch of people going around doing that."],[1124.13,"Uh he says, especially from those of the circumcision, meaning um there are a lot of, because you have two kinds of people who are in the Christian community."],[1134.29,"You have people who are Judeans, who already are very familiar with the Hebrew Bible."],[1141.69,"And who have received all of these traditions, and then you have former pagans."],[1147.03,"And the former pagans know a whole lot less, about doctrine and everything."],[1151.94,"And so he's saying some of these people, because they come out of this Judean background, and have this knowledge, they're wanting to sort of take these leadership roles."],[1165.54,"And draw them after themselves."],[1166.98,"And we know Second Temple Judaism, Judaism in this period is divided up into all kinds of different sects."],[1174.68,"Just you see the Pharisees, Sadducees, Scribes, Herodians, just in Judea itself in the Gospels."],[1180.85,"And as you spread out around the world, there's tons of them."],[1184.39,"So this is what St."],[1185.59,"Paul means when he says they're drawing off like whole households, is they're sort of doing, well, we're gonna do Pharisee Christianity and we're gonna be do, you know, bringing their own bent to it."],[1196.51,"And St. Paul's saying that' thats g'onnas gonna tear this community apart."],[1200.57,"So we need to have one bishop who appoints elders, presbyters, priests in each of these cities."],[1210.25,"So there's this clear connection back to St."],[1212.65,"Paul, back to an apostle."],[1214.94,"Because we have a clear connection back to an apostle, then they automatically, when someone comes in wanting to teach whatever, that's not connected to an. issue in the church since the first century"],[1234.16,"We just got it all figured out in the first century."],[1238.0,"Nobody has shown up trying to draw off a following after themselves or coming up with weird doctrines."],[1241.52,"It's all been good"],[1247.78,"But so I want to reiterate."],[1251.08,"St. Paul's answer here to that problem is not, well, you just need to go deeper into your scripture scholarship."],[1265.18,"We need rabbis who have really mastered the text and who can resolve these disputes."],[1271.34,"That is not what he says."],[1273.75,"What he says is we need ordained individuals in the church with a connection to the apostles."],[1283.91,"Apostolic succession, if you will."],[1287.09,"We need that to establish what is true."],[1291.33,"Because what is true is not the correct interpretation of scriptures."],[1296.55,"What is true is not a system of systematic theology and a system of doctrine."],[1302.95,"What is true is the gospel that was preached by the apostles."],[1307.83,"Because it's the gospel preached by the apostles that manifest Christ."],[1312.07,"And Christ is the truth."],[1314.39,"And the only way to make sure you have that is if you have this direct connection to the apostles."],[1320.15,"Irenaeus of Lyon famously takes this very seriously"],[1320.29,"And so St."],[1326.19,"He says, if you have any questions. about doctrine, he doesn't say, become an expert on scripture"],[1332.76,"And he doesn't say, uh, look at look at canons."],[1337.48,"He doesn't say, look at the writings of this or that previous father."],[1343.8,"He says, look at the churches that are directly connected to the apostles."],[1349.96,"And he gives lists of the bishops going back to the apostles in these churches."],[1355.06,"He says there's still when he was writing, I mean, he's writing in the second century."],[1359.38,"So he's like, we're still kind of in the living memory."],[1361.62,"We're just a couple generations away from the apostles."],[1364.58,"We're still a living memory."],[1365.86,"That living memory is still preserved in these churches."],[1368.26,"Look at what they're teaching."],[1370.66,"So when Valentinus shows up teaching you some crazy Gnosticism about the Ogduad and stuff."],[1377.31,"Like you can and he claims that's what the apostles taught."],[1382.13,"You say, Well, wait a minute."],[1383.65,"Rome and Antioch and Jerusalem, they still have the living memory of the apostles."],[1389.57,"They're governed over and are taught by these men who are appointed by men who are appointed by the apostles."],[1396.87,"They're the ones who still have what the apostles taught."],[1400.79,"They're the place where Christ is still manifest for people who are looking for salvation."],[1406.47,"And Saint Irenaeus is getting that from the apostles teaching it."],[1411.27,"And here's a place where St."],[1412.47,"Paul is teaching it."],[1417.71,"So that's worth hammering a little bit."],[1422.35,"So then he goes on, verse 12."],[1427.55,"One of them, a prophet of their own, said, Cretans are always liars, evil beasts, lazy gluttons."],[1435.15,"This testimony is true."],[1438.91,"This is where we get the idea of calling somebody a Cretan if they're dull without any."],[1445.35,"It's spelled a little differently."],[1447.67,"But it is true that in the ancient world no one liked Cretans."],[1452.71,"I can't repeat for purposes of ancient faith and mixed company what Plato says about Cretans in On the Laws, but you can go look that up yourself."],[1465.19,"Yeah. And I'm not sure why, because that's like 400 years before this, and already they're talking trash on Cretans."],[1475.75,"But uh yeah, they were not considered."],[1479.35,"In fact, that that uh that evil beasts uh part may be related to what Plato said."],[1488.07,"But um yeah."],[1492.31,"Um but uh and apologies to any Cretans who might hear this, but I'm just reading what St."],[1500.15,"Paul said. Um so he and and apparently he's quoting when he says this some Cretan author."],[1509.36,"So he's kind of saying they even kind of realize, you know, you know."],[1514.48,"So I guess this is like the equivalent of quoting George Carlin talking about Americans being lazy and out of shape and you know addicted to television or something."],[1525.68,"Um yeah, so this test works true."],[1532.4,"Therefore, rebuke them sharply that they may be sound in the faith."],[1536.58,"Or sound in faithfulness."],[1538.26,"So the answer to this, so you know, easy to think St"],[1543.22,"Paul's a racist."],[1544.42,"How dare he."],[1545.59,"Um the point isn't. that's what these people are like, so they're hopeless, leave"],[1555.69,"The idea is that that's what these people have been like."],[1559.53,"This is why they need the gospel."],[1561.13,"This is why they need Christ."],[1562.91,"And therefore, because this is the history there, because the culture there is in disrepair, you need to rebuke and correct them and teach them."],[1574.03,"Because they need to become faithful, they need to change, they need to repent."],[1578.68,"And follow Christ."],[1579.72,"That's what we want for them."],[1581.46,"That's that's what you're trying to do."],[1584.02,"Um, but he's so what he's saying here is not like a racist thing, it's more you've got your work cut out for you there."],[1591.96,"This is not like you're not going to a uh a sort of uh stuffy, highly moral."],[1604.38,"This isn't Victorian England."],[1606.16,"That he's going to to try and organize churches."],[1609.44,"This is a very different type of place."],[1616.56,"Verse 14 not giving heed to Jewish fables and commandments of men who turn from the truth."],[1623.68,"Uh, yeah, I don't know, Jewish Judean fables."],[1626.96,"But um, this is more of that what I was talking about with these people from from a Judean background who are bringing this stuff from their sectarian, their various sectarian backgrounds."],[1641.25,"That's what the fables would be."],[1643.43,"Because every one of those had their own set of literature beyond the scriptures."],[1648.53,"And their own set of rules, different dietary rules and fastings and washings and all of these things."],[1655.47,"And so he's saying, what those people need, you've got your work cut out for you, and what they need is the gospel, not all this other nonsense."],[1667.41,"That other nonsense is not what's going to help them find salvation."],[1672.25,"And so you need to not get distracted by that."],[1676.19,"You need to focus on bringing them to faithfulness."],[1682.35,"Verse 15 To the pure all things are pure, but to those who are defiled and unbelieving, nothing is pure."],[1689.63,"But even their mind and conscience are defiled."],[1692.99,"They profess to know God, but in works they deny him, being abominable, disobedient, and disqualified for every good work."],[1708.47,"To the pure, all things are pure, is probably him referencing something they say, maybe."],[1715.43,"Um it's hard to tell exactly because that doesn't seem to be a maxim from anywhere else."],[1722.57,"So maybe this is this is uh something they're saying, and he's kind of turning it around on them."],[1728.83,"So that they're they're teaching some kind of like there's somebody there teaching some kind of lawlessness."],[1734.29,"Like if you're pure, then you can do whatever you want, kind of idea."],[1739.01,"Um, and so he's then countering that by saying, uh, yeah, but if you're defiled, then everything is defiled."],[1746.93,"Everything about you is defiled, your mind is defiled."],[1749.9,"Everything is corrupt."],[1752.3,"Umtice that language of disqualified for every good work."],[1759.02,"That's actually the reverse of what he said in 2 Timothy uh 3 ,16. and 17 that we read last time"],[1766.98,"We talked about the scripture making you fully prepared for every good work."],[1775.14,"They're the opposite situation."],[1777.06,"They're not prepared to do any good work."],[1782.34,"And he talked, uses that language again other form of godliness, meaning they look like they're these super pious people because they have all these rules and they have all this knowledge of this arcane stuff."],[1795.04,"But when you look at their works, when you look at the fruit of what their life produces, it's not producing salvation."],[1801.84,"And the uh the Cretans, your average pagan Cretan out there is already defiled"],[1808.63,"He needs to find purity, and you're gonna find that through faithfulness to Christ."],[1812.95,"They don't need to get further defiled."],[1820.04,"So chapter 2, verse 1."],[1822.6,"But as for you, speak the things which are proper for sound doctrine, that the older men be sober, reverent, temperate, sound in faith, in love, in patience, the older women likewise, that they be reverent in behavior, not slanderers, not given to much wine, teachers of good things, that they admonish the young women to love their husbands, to love their children, to be discreet, chaste, housemakers, good, obedient to their own husbands, that the word of God may not be blasphemed."],[1857.2,"We'll pause there."],[1858.64,"So notice something interesting."],[1862.0,"He says, as for you, speak the things which are proper for sound doctrine."],[1867.04,"So you expect, oh, he's gonna list a bunch of doctrines."],[1872.16,"That's not what he lists."],[1876.52,"When he describes the doctrines of the faith, it's a bunch of works."],[1881.0,"It's almost as if those aren't two different things."],[1884.24,"Um he said sarcastically."],[1887.2,"Um but right so what does it mean?"],[1891.08,"What does it mean?"],[1892.12,"Because he's just said the way you know that the doctrines that these false teachers, these sectarian teachers are teaching, the way you know that they're false, the evidence that they're false, is the fruit that it bears in their lives, their behavior, their works."],[1911.47,"So then, if you're teaching them the true doctrine, if they're coming to know Christ, if they're living life faithful to Christ, that's going to produce a different kind of fruit."],[1922.43,"What are those fruit?"],[1923.87,"These things that St."],[1924.91,"Paul is listing."],[1927.31,"So he starts with the older men, the s these are going to be and women, the established members of the community."],[1933.6,"And and all these behaviors that we've seen him, again, first and second Timothy, it was these same kind of things."],[1939.2,"And that the older men and women have sort of a mentorship role"],[1944.74,"Even within the church."],[1946.51,"Beyond the people who are presbyters and deacons."],[1951.33,"There's the older men and women and the younger men and women."],[1954.95,"So there's this structure, even of that."],[1957.69,"Where the older men and women are living lives as examples to the younger men and women and serving in this mentorship kind of role."],[1966.01,"So the young men in the church should be able to look to the older men in the church and see what a Christian man looks like."],[1972.99,"And how what they need to grow into."],[1975.15,"Likewise, with women."],[1976.27,"That's why it talks about the older women talking to the younger women."],[1980.16,"Because St. Paul realizes that there are things women know about being a woman that men don't know."],[1988.36,"And there are things about being a man that women don't know."],[1994.12,"And so, and so there is a, again, as we've talked about before, people want to say St."],[2001.16,"Paul's this weird misogynist, but he sees this role."],[2004.52,"There is female leadership for St."],[2007.16,"Paul in churches."],[2009.02,"They have this necessary function."],[2012.78,"Because at least half the church, in the early church, it was way more than half the church, is women."],[2019.04,"It's other women."],[2020.96,"He sees this while he's describing it"],[2021.8,"So this is part of what St."],[2023.88,"Paul is talking about here."],[2027.72,"He's describing it."],[2034.76,"Verse 6, likewise exhort the young men to be sober-minded."],[2039.32,"Not that young men are ever not sober-minded."],[2043.08,"In all things showing yourself to be a pattern of good works, in doctrine showing integrity, reverence, incorruptibility, sound speech that cannot be condemned, that one who is an opponent may be ashamed, having nothing evil to say of you."],[2059.08,"So when he's talking about the young men, he tells St."],[2062.52,"Titus how to act."],[2067.64,"He's saying you need to have this mentorship role with the young men in particular"],[2073.66,"You notice he didn't say the young men and women."],[2077.34,"He's already talked about the older women helping the younger women."],[2081.1,"We talk about this a little more when we talked about deaconesses back in 1 Timothy."],[2087.72,"So that there is this role."],[2088.84,"But so Saint Titus, even more than, because of his leadership role, even more than the other older men in the community, needs to have this kind of mentorship role with the young men in the church."],[2103.81,"Verse 9. Exhort bondservants to be obedient to their own masters, to be well pleasing in all things, not answering back, not pilfering, but showing all all good fidelity, that they may adorn the doctrine of God our Savior in things."],[2119.29,"So this is completely non-controversial."],[2124.01,"This is one of the places where St."],[2125.53,"Paul basically says, slaves obey your masters."],[2129.1,"And when we get to the introduction to Philemon here in a little bit, I'm going to say a lot more about St."],[2133.5,"Paul and slavery."],[2134.54,"So I'll save most of this until then."],[2136.46,"But notice what he's saying here."],[2138.14,"He's saying that someone who is an enslaved person."],[2143.88,"Who who here's the preaching of St."],[2147.16,"Paul, here's the preaching of St."],[2148.6,"Titus. Why are they to conduct themselves in this way?"],[2152.26,"First of all, they're to conduct themselves as a Christian."],[2156.82,"It's not this is not like a radically different thing"],[2157.08,"The way free people conduct themselves as Christians."],[2162.73,"That he's telling them to do."],[2165.13,"But why? The goal here, is that they be a witness to the doctrine of God, that they be a witness of Christ to the person who's enslaved them."],[2184.91,"So the focus here is not just, hey, no slave rebellions."],[2192.72,"Uh they never work unless you're in Haiti, and that hasn't gone so well either."],[2199.44,"So um it's not just no slave rebellions, but why no slave rebellions?"],[2205.32,"Because slavery's a good thing."],[2208.76,"St. Paul doesn't think that."],[2211.02,"He says as much."],[2212.48,"There's no slave rebellions because our goal is not to decide who's the good guy and who's the bad guy."],[2221.68,"Who's the oppressor and who's the victim?"],[2225.12,"And once we've decided who the oppressor is, we tell him quite literally to go to hell."],[2231.45,"And we just care about the victim and we try to bring Christ to them."],[2236.67,"That's not how St."],[2238.51,"Paul views it."],[2239.87,"St. Paul wants to bring Christ to the victim and to the oppressor."],[2242.35,"And more than that, St"],[2247.27,"Paul sees how, you know, he's been around enough and has been involved in the murder of enough people back before Christ appeared to him."],[2255.59,"That he knows that every one of us in our lives are at some points the victim of other people and at other points victimize other people and harm other people."],[2267.43,"We're harmed and we harm people."],[2269.91,"We're sinned against and we sin against people."],[2273.91,"And so there's not this easy line of good person, bad person."],[2278.63,"We bring the gospel to the good person and abominate the bad person."],[2284.01,"So we're all created good but dominated by sin."],[2286.57,"And God wants salvation for every single one of us"],[2294.45,"And St. Paul sees, as he said to when he was talking about wives obeying their husbands, he saw that a Christian wife may be the means by which the pagan husband ends up coming to know Christ."],[2309.07,"Even if while he's still a pagan, he's a jerk."],[2312.77,"And is horrible to her and mistreats her and abuses her."],[2318.37,"Likewise. The slaves who, like women, were coming in droves to Christianity in the first couple of centuries, uh, could be the means by which their often wealthy masters might come to know Christ."],[2339.65,"And St. Paul, remember, the whole reason he's there in Rome is he's trying to get to Caesar to try to convert Caesar."],[2345.57,"I mean, Caesar's not only an oppressor, Caesar's essentially the Antichrist."],[2351.17,"Like, quite literally, at this point in history."],[2354.15,"This Nero we're talking about."],[2356.39,"Uh you know, and St."],[2358.79,"Paul's goal is unlike a lot of his fellow Judeans who were zealots, who were about to touch off a series of rebellions that was going to get their province destroyed, uh, he was not trying to overthrow Caesar."],[2373.19,"He was not trying to start a revolution against Caesar."],[2376.19,"He was trying to bring Caesar to Christ."],[2380.27,"That ended up happening 250 years later, but that's but St."],[2384.83,"Paul was trying for it here."],[2387.09,"That's always the goal for St."],[2388.53,"Paul. And so that's why he tells enslaved people to conduct themselves in this way."],[2395.65,"In the hope of bringing others within that household to Christ."],[2406.69,"Verse 11 for the grace of God that brings salvation has appeared to all men."],[2412.93,"Talk about Christ again, Christ manifesting himself, that theme from all the way back in the beginning."],[2419.27,"Teaching us that denying ungodliness and worldly lusts, we should live soberly, righteously, and godly in the present age, looking for the blessed hope and glorious appearing of our great God and Savior Jesus Christ, who gave himself for us, that he might redeem us from every lawless deed and purify for himself his own special people, zealous for good works."],[2442.47,"I like the old King James translation better, that where it's his own peculiar people."],[2448.99,"Which is an older thing of peculiar, but some of us are rather peculiar."],[2454.11,"Yeah. Some of us are rather peculiar in all senses."],[2461.63,"So notice how."],[2465.63,"I know I'm probably beating a mostly dead horse."],[2468.83,"Uh for the people in this room, uh, not for the United States of America."],[2474.91,"But notice how for St."],[2476.67,"Paul, when he communicates the gospel here."],[2482.24,"The gospel, what Christ did, he's very clear about it."],[2485.84,"Christ gave himself for us, he died for us, he redeemed us from every lawless deed, he redeemed us from sin."],[2492.45,"But that has a purpose."],[2495.41,"He did that so that we would bear fruit."],[2498.45,"He did that so we would live in a certain way, so that we would be zealous for good works."],[2505.41,"Those two things aren't separate."],[2506.85,"aren't Those two separate things."],[2507.49,"This is all one thing"],[2513.83,"And this has been his theme throughout this epistle, as we've seen."],[2517.43,"The one produces the other."],[2518.95,"If you have the correct doctrine, if you know Christ."],[2523.01,"If you know Christ, that will produce this fruit in your life."],[2528.59,"And so that is part and parcel of salvation."],[2531.87,"It's inseparable from salvation."],[2538.43,"And notice how St."],[2539.71,"Paul sort of front loads here the way of life part."],[2548.8,"Christ has appeared to all men teaching us that."],[2550.0,"So what's for the people who say St"],[2556.52,"Paul never talks about Christ's teaching in the Gospels?"],[2559.65,"He summarizes it here."],[2564.77,"The Gospels aren't just the Passion narrative."],[2570.77,"The Gospels are Christ teaching us how to live, then the passion narrative, then him giving himself for us."],[2580.53,"Then him rising again and sending us with a purpose out into the world to do good works."],[2586.93,"So St Paul's summary of the gospel here in these few verses is basically a condensation of of the structure the synoptic gospels."],[2598.89,"Just done very briefly."],[2606.17,"Verse 15. Speak these things, exhort and rebuke all with rebuke with all authority."],[2613.77,"Let no one despise you."],[2616.65,"So this, this is it."],[2618.95,"This is what you need to again and again tell them."],[2622.81,"And correct them."],[2624.78,"And do it with authority."],[2631.18,"Chapter 3, verse 1."],[2633.02,"Remind them to be subject to rulers and authorities, to obey,y to be, to be ready for ever good work, to speak evil of no one peaceable, gentle, showing all humility to all men."],[2646.9,"So again, he's not saying something different to everyone else than he did to enslaved people."],[2655.72,"Because this is the Roman Empire."],[2657.4,"There's a hierarchy."],[2659.0,"Unless you're Caesar, there's somebody over you."],[2662.26,"There's somebody above you."],[2663.94,"There's somebody in charge."],[2666.28,"Even if you're not enslaved."],[2668.12,"And so you need to be subject to them and obedient to them in the same way."],[2673.5,"Ready for evil work, speak evil of no one, be peaceful and gentle, and showing all humility."],[2677.66,"Why? For the same reason."],[2679.82,"Verse 3 To try to help bring them to Christ."],[2687.06,"For we ourselves were also once foolish, disobedient, deceived, serving various lusts and pleasures, living in malice and envy, hateful and hating one another."],[2698.18,"But when the kindness and the love of God, our Savior, toward man appeared, Christ again."],[2704.03,"When Christ appeared, we were all that way."],[2707.33,"All those people out there who you don't want to be kind to."],[2711.17,"All those people out there who you don't want to be obedient to, who you don't want to be humble around."],[2716.79,"All those people, we were like that too."],[2720.55,"We were just as bad, if not worse, before Christ. appeared"],[2727.15,"Verse 5 Not by works of righteousness, which we have done, but according to his mercy, he saved us through the washing of regeneration and renewing of the Holy Spirit."],[2737.84,"There was nothing that caught that we did that caused Christ to appear."],[2744.24,"That caused Christ to manifest himself to us through Paul's St."],[2748.64,"Paul's preaching. Or in general, in the incarnation."],[2753.22,"Why does he need to make that point?"],[2755.14,"St. Paul is a Pharisee."],[2757.55,"What did the Pharisees think about the exile?"],[2760.67,"How did the Pharisees operate?"],[2762.35,"The whole MO of the Pharisees as a sect was that look, disobedience got us exiled."],[2771.11,"So if we all obey, and if we can get everyone in Judea to obey the law perfectly, then the Messiah will come."],[2780.79,"Then the exile will end."],[2782.47,"Then salvation will come."],[2785.77,"And so anybody who was a sinner had to be rooted out of the community."],[2788.89,"That's why they hated sinners."],[2789.93,"They hated the tax collection."],[2791.29,"They wanted to stone them to death, get rid of them."],[2794.06,"Because they're keeping, they're what's keeping us in exile."],[2799.44,"So they rather than, and this is a big part of every interaction between Christ and the Pharisees, is that they had externalized what should have been internal."],[2809.28,"They should have been within themselves trying to root out the sin and repent of it."],[2810.48,"And instead, they had externalized it"],[2815.88,"They had put the sin onto other people to try to remove them from the community."],[2821.64,"So St. Paul is directly countering here that Pharisaic narrative."],[2827.16,"Christ didn't appear because we finally got our act together."],[2831.24,"Just like the Exodus from Egypt didn't happen because all the enslaved Israelites just became really good."],[2839.32,"And so God decided I'm gonna."],[2843.72,"It was in God's own timing."],[2845.88,"And he did it, and he purified us us. And set free"],[2852.86,"Verse 6, talking about the Holy Spirit, whom he poured out on us abundantly through Jesus Christ our Savior, that having been justified by his grace, we should become heirs according to the hope of eternal life."],[2867.47,"So we're justified."],[2869.55,"Remember, justification is like the way we use it in word processing."],[2874.11,"You're put in order."],[2875.33,"Left justified. Just full judgment."],[2877.83,"You're put in order, set straight."],[2879.91,"So by grace, by God's action, we were put back in order."],[2884.63,"We were renewed."],[2885.67,"We're no longer what we were before."],[2889.13,"And we've been filled with the Holy Spirit, and we've been become heirs of the promise."],[2894.73,"What promise? The promises to Abraham of eternal life."],[2898.25,"And so now we've been brought into that"],[2898.27,"Sharing in God's life."],[2904.59,"Right? And implying what?"],[2907.47,"So can those people."],[2910.59,"So could those people who are so miserable and defiled and sinful now."],[2914.92,"Through Christ. Verse 8."],[2918.84,"This is a faithful saying, and these things I want you to affirm constantly that those who have believed in God should be careful to maintain good works."],[2927.88,"These things are good and profitable to men."],[2932.04,"Now, as soon as he gets done saying that that Pharisaic narrative is wrong."],[2939.09,"He immediately wants to add, lest someone misunderstand that in Germany in 1517, that those who have received that grace from God, whom God says, need to what?"],[2955.89,"Be careful to maintain good works."],[2968.85,"You might become out of order again."],[2972.37,"You might return to that defilement."],[2974.53,"You might return to that life of sin."],[2977.27,"And then not experience the hope of eternal life."],[2984.95,"I don't know."],[2986.43,"I don't think so."],[2988.43,"I don't think so."],[2994.99,"Verse nine. But avoid foolish disputes, genealogies, contentions, and strivings about the law, for they are unprofitable and useless."],[3004.93,"So again, those sectarians, who are coming around."],[3010.04,"Don't even argue with them about it."],[3012.28,"Don't wade into the right the muck of their detailed internet debates."],[3017.82,"Just like let it be."],[3020.11,"Because there's nothing good is gonna come out."],[3021.95,"They're unprofitable and useless."],[3024.83,"Reject a divisive man after the first and second admonition. knowing that such a person is warped and sinning, being self-condemned"],[3033.81,"So three strikes, you're out."],[3036.71,"So the first time they're doing this nonsense, you don't get in and argue with them."],[3040.95,"The first warning."],[3042.64,"You say, hey, you need to knock this off."],[3044.88,"Second warning, hey, you need to dock this off."],[3047.28,"Third time, you're out."],[3049.86,"You're out of the community."],[3051.56,"Until you change your behavior."],[3057.16,"Verse 12 When I send Artemis to you, or Tychicus, be diligent to come to me at Nicopolis, for I've decided to spend the winter there."],[3066.68,"Send Zenus the lawyer and Apollos on their journey with haste, that they may lack nothing, and let our people also learn to maintain good works, to meet urgent needs, that they may not be unfruitful."],[3078.32,"All who are with me greet you, greet those who love us in the faith."],[3082.0,"Grace be with you all."],[3083.84,"Amen. Well, he's hoping."],[3094.0,"He's hoping. Um the geography doesn't work out."],[3098.08,"That's why I say that."],[3099.92,"That he ever managed to get back there."],[3103.36,"Um this is where is my couple?"],[3107.6,"Um well, there's a bunch of them."],[3112.16,"Victory Town. Yeah, city of the Vtoricy."],[3115.12,"Yeah Yeah. So we're not sure exactly which one, but there's not any of them that work out."],[3126.42,"For when this would have been written."],[3128.9,"So this is presumably at an earlier point than 1st and 2 Timothy when he's thinking he's still gonna maybe continue."],[3141.75,"And you notice he didn't make any direct comments about his passing the way he did in it 2 Timothy."],[3149.67,"So he talks about, he says, basically, stay there and do this work in Crete until I send, I'm gonna send somebody, Artemis or Tickets."],[3158.87,"I'll send somebody to come get you and then come back here."],[3160.55,"I'll say hi to them for you"],[3161.63,"Everybody here says hi."],[3167.55,"Um, sort of his usual ending."],[3169.63,"But notice that note he ends on let our people also learn to maintain good works to meet urgent needs, that they may not be unfruitful."],[3179.39,"This idea of producing fruit is for him part and parcel of the gospel."],[3188.31,"Okay, thus ends uh St."],[3193.43,"Paul's Epistle to Titus."],[3195.83,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[3203.19,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[3204.63,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaithcom."]],"o":[{"t":22.56,"lv":2,"h":"Intro: Titus (and Philemon tonight)","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Tonight <b>two books</b>, both very short: <b>Titus</b> and <b>Philemon</b> (\"like a paragraph\")</li><li class=\"d1\">Ancient Faith will probably <b>snip in half</b>, two releases - so introduces each separately</li><li class=\"d1\">joke: could do both intros now - \"poor <b>Tim Wenger</b>\" who edits</li><li class=\"d0\">Titus intro short - \"<b>dittoing</b>\" 1 and 2 Timothy</li><li class=\"d1\">will repeat a lot of <b>1 Timothy</b> in particular</li><li class=\"d1\">written around the same time: Paul in his <b>second Roman imprisonment</b>, knows end is near</li></ul>"},{"t":138.48,"lv":3,"h":"Pastoral epistles and the episcopacy","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Pastoral epistles</b> = 1 &amp; 2 Timothy and Titus - Paul's <b>mentorship role</b></li><li class=\"d1\">preparing them for <b>expanded ministry</b></li><li class=\"d1\">the office we now call <b>bishop</b> - the <i>episcopacy</i> of 1 Timothy</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul and other apostles soon <b>no longer with them in the flesh</b> → transition to <b>another era</b> of the church</li><li class=\"d0\">Objection: offices <i>episkopos</i>, <i>presbyteros</i>, deacon = <b>later period</b></li><li class=\"d1\">answer: while <b>apostles alive</b>, little difference between presbyter and bishop (= what we'd call a priest)</li><li class=\"d1\">once apostles gone → <b>formalized</b>: bishops = those <b>designated by the apostles</b> to carry on</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Timothy and Titus</b> two of those</li></ul>"},{"t":239.08,"lv":3,"h":"Who Titus is","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Titus less prominent than Timothy</li><li class=\"d1\">Timothy came up repeatedly, listed as <b>co-writer</b>; not Titus</li><li class=\"d0\">Circle around Paul: <b>Luke, Mark, Silas, Barnabas</b></li><li class=\"d1\">others from churches, usually numbered among the <b>70</b> - like <b>Priscilla and Aquila</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Titus one of these</li><li class=\"d0\">Letter shorter: relationship <b>not quite as close</b> as with Timothy → explains differences</li><li class=\"d1\">similarities: preparing him for <b>similar ministry</b></li></ul>"},{"t":328.81,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: where were they bishops?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: do we know where Timothy and Titus were bishops?</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Timothy</b> went out from <b>Ephesus</b>, was at several places</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Titus</b> in <b>Crete</b> - comes up right in the letter; Paul assigns him there</li><li class=\"d1\">Titus = obviously a <b>Roman name</b></li></ul>"},{"t":371.33,"lv":2,"h":"Titus 1:1-3 - Greeting; slave and apostle","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:1</b> \"Paul, a <b>bond servant</b> of God and an <b>apostle</b> of Jesus Christ\"</li><li class=\"d1\">usual intro - but already <b>seeding themes</b></li><li class=\"d1\">bond servant = <b>slave</b>; apostle = one <b>sent</b> by Christ</li><li class=\"d0\">\"according to the <b>faith</b> of God's elect\" - better <b>faithfulness</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"acknowledgement of the truth which accords with <b>godliness</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">covering <b>both ends</b>: servant of God, sent by Christ - and the <b>godly in the churches</b> he visited testify to his ministry</li><li class=\"d1\">attested from <b>human side and God's side</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Elect = same as 1 and 2 Timothy: the <b>remnant, the faithful</b></li></ul>"},{"t":495.88,"lv":3,"h":"1:2-3 - Promised before time began","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:2</b> \"in hope of <b>eternal life</b>, which God who cannot lie promised <b>before time began</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">eternal life = the <b>goal</b>; promised to whom? - point is <b>God's purpose all along</b></li><li class=\"d1\">why he created humanity: to <b>share his life</b> eternally</li><li class=\"d1\">not <b>plan B</b> after Adam and Eve's sin</li><li class=\"d1\">he can't lie → <b>sure the whole time</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Eternal things <b>enter human experience</b> at a point; we find out</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul: Christ the <b>Lamb slain before the foundation of the world</b> - yet slain ~30 AD</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>both true</b>: eternal reality entering history on a particular day</li><li class=\"d1\">plan = <b>mystery</b>, revealed in these latter days</li></ul>"},{"t":652.4,"lv":3,"h":"\"Word\" manifested through preaching","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"manifested his <b>word</b> through preaching\" - should be <b>capital W</b></li><li class=\"d1\">lowercase makes people think <b>the Bible</b> - doesn't make sense: his preaching just started, Old Testament written centuries before</li><li class=\"d1\">= <b>Christ</b>, the way the mystery is manifest</li><li class=\"d0\">How is Christ manifest to <b>Titus and the Gentile communities</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">Judeans saw him in person; Gentiles see him through <b>Paul's preaching</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"committed to me according to the commandment of <b>God our Savior</b>\" = the <b>gospel</b> committed to him</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:4</b> \"to Titus, a <b>true son</b> in our common faith\"</li><li class=\"d1\">with Timothy: \"my son\", direct <b>father-son</b>; Titus a son <b>of the church</b> - fatherly/patronal, less intimate</li><li class=\"d1\">grace, mercy, peace - typical intro</li></ul>"},{"t":797.04,"lv":2,"h":"1:5 - Left in Crete to appoint elders","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:5</b> \"I <b>left you in Crete</b>... set in order what is lacking, <b>appoint elders in every city</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul had been there; Titus told to <b>stay</b> - not sent</li><li class=\"d0\">Argument: this must be a <b>late stage</b> church, ~100 years on</li><li class=\"d1\">\"you don't plant a church and <b>wait a hundred years to ask for a priest</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">any sizable community wants <b>order and structure</b> at once</li><li class=\"d1\">implies the opposite: gospel just preached in Crete, communities in the cities, Paul wants Titus to <b>organize</b> them</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Presbyter = elder</b> - not made up for a new religion</li><li class=\"d1\">from the <b>Old Testament</b>: elders of the tribes; <i>presbyteros</i> in the Greek OT; every tribe and clan had them</li><li class=\"d1\">Christian communities structured like Israel's → a <b>continuation</b>, not \"we're done with that\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Titus appoints/ordains elders (priests) → their <b>supervisor</b> = <i>episkopos</i> = <b>bishop</b></li><li class=\"d1\">needs six or nine months after preaching - not a century</li></ul>"},{"t":995.9,"lv":3,"h":"1:6-9 - Qualifications for bishop","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:6-9</b> blameless, <b>husband of one wife</b>, faithful children, not self-willed, not quick-tempered, not given to wine, not violent, not greedy; hospitable, sober-minded, just, holy, self-controlled</li><li class=\"d1\">holding fast the <b>faithful word</b> as taught → exhort by <b>sound doctrine</b> and convict those who contradict</li><li class=\"d0\">Very like <b>1 Timothy</b> - condensed, not quite verbatim</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul doesn't change requirements for ordained leaders <b>depending on where you are</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1054.42,"lv":2,"h":"1:10-11 - Why clear leadership matters","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:10-11</b> many <b>insubordinate</b>, idle talkers, deceivers - esp. <b>those of the circumcision</b>; mouths must be stopped; subvert whole households for dishonest gain</li><li class=\"d0\">Why leadership <b>directly connected to an apostle</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">many want to show up, teach, <b>start movements</b> in newborn communities</li><li class=\"d1\">again suggests <b>not a long time</b> - leadership would have evolved naturally</li><li class=\"d1\">nascent group, new to gospel, little doctrine → <b>people turn up and draw followings</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Circumcision\" = two kinds in the church: <b>Judeans</b> who know the Hebrew Bible and traditions, and <b>former pagans</b> who know far less</li><li class=\"d1\">Judeans want leadership roles, draw people after themselves</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Second Temple Judaism</b> split into sects: Pharisees, Sadducees, scribes, Herodians - tons more abroad</li><li class=\"d1\">drawing off households = \"<b>Pharisee Christianity</b>\", each with own bent → will tear the community apart</li><li class=\"d0\">Need <b>one bishop</b> who appoints elders in each city → clear connection back to <b>Paul, an apostle</b></li><li class=\"d1\">someone comes teaching something not connected → recognized</li><li class=\"d1\">\"issue in the church since the <b>first century</b>\" - sarcasm: \"we got it all figured out in the first century\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1247.78,"lv":3,"h":"Apostolic succession, not scholarship","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul's answer is <b>not</b>: go deeper in scripture scholarship, get <b>rabbis</b> who master the text</li><li class=\"d1\">answer = <b>ordained individuals</b> with a connection to the apostles - <b>apostolic succession</b></li><li class=\"d1\">what is true is <b>not</b> correct interpretation, not systematic theology or doctrinal system</li><li class=\"d1\">true = the <b>gospel preached by the apostles</b> → manifests <b>Christ</b>, who is the truth</li><li class=\"d1\">only way to be sure: <b>direct connection to the apostles</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Irenaeus of Lyon</b> takes this seriously</li><li class=\"d1\">questions about doctrine: not \"become an expert on scripture\", not canons, not earlier fathers</li><li class=\"d1\">→ look at <b>churches directly connected to the apostles</b>; lists of bishops back to the apostles</li><li class=\"d1\">2nd century - \"living memory\", a couple generations away</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Valentinus</b> with Gnosticism (the <i>Ogdoad</i>) claiming apostolic teaching → Rome, Antioch, Jerusalem still have the memory, taught by men appointed by men appointed by apostles</li><li class=\"d1\">places where <b>Christ is still manifest</b> for those seeking salvation</li><li class=\"d1\">Irenaeus got it from the apostles teaching it; here Paul teaching it - \"worth hammering\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1422.35,"lv":2,"h":"1:12-13 - Cretans always liars","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:12</b> \"a prophet of their own\": Cretans <b>always liars, evil beasts, lazy gluttons</b>; \"this testimony is true\"</li><li class=\"d1\">source of calling someone a \"<b>Cretan</b>\" for dull (spelled differently)</li><li class=\"d1\">no one liked Cretans in the ancient world - <b>Plato, <i>On the Laws</i></b> (won't repeat it) ~400 years earlier</li><li class=\"d1\">\"evil beasts\" maybe related to Plato</li><li class=\"d1\">apologies to Cretans - quoting a <b>Cretan author</b>; like quoting <b>George Carlin</b> on Americans</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:13</b> \"rebuke them sharply that they may be <b>sound in the faith</b>\" (faithfulness)</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul a racist? No - not \"hopeless, leave\"</li><li class=\"d1\">that's what they've been like → <b>why they need Christ</b>; culture in disrepair → rebuke, correct, teach</li><li class=\"d1\">need to <b>repent and follow Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"you've got your work cut out\" - <b>not Victorian England</b>, a very different place to organize churches</li></ul>"},{"t":1616.56,"lv":3,"h":"1:14-16 - Fables and defilement","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:14</b> \"Jewish fables and commandments of men\"</li><li class=\"d1\">people from <b>Judean sectarian backgrounds</b>; each sect had literature beyond scripture, own rules - <b>diet, fastings, washings</b></li><li class=\"d1\">what they need is the <b>gospel</b>, not nonsense; don't get distracted → bring them to <b>faithfulness</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:15</b> \"To the pure all things are pure...\"</li><li class=\"d1\">probably something <b>they say</b> - not a known maxim; Paul turns it around</li><li class=\"d1\">lawlessness: \"if you're pure, do whatever\" → but if defiled, <b>mind and conscience defiled</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"<b>disqualified for every good work</b>\" - reverse of <b>2 Timothy 3:16-17</b> (scripture → fully prepared for every good work)</li><li class=\"d1\">\"form of godliness\" - pious look: rules, arcane knowledge → <b>fruit of life</b> doesn't produce salvation</li><li class=\"d1\">average pagan Cretan already defiled - finds purity through <b>faithfulness to Christ</b>, not more defilement</li></ul>"},{"t":1820.04,"lv":2,"h":"2:1-5 - Sound doctrine = a way of life","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:1-5</b> speak what fits <b>sound doctrine</b>: older men sober, reverent, temperate, sound in faith, love, patience; older women reverent, not slanderers, <b>teachers of good things</b>, admonish young women to love husbands and children, discreet, chaste, housemakers</li><li class=\"d1\">\"that the word of God may not be <b>blasphemed</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Expect a list of <b>doctrines</b> - he lists <b>works</b>: \"almost as if those aren't two different things\"</li><li class=\"d1\">false teachers' doctrine shown false by the <b>fruit in their lives</b></li><li class=\"d1\">true doctrine, knowing Christ → different fruit; these are those fruits</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Older men and women</b> = established members, <b>mentorship role</b></li><li class=\"d1\">beyond presbyters and deacons: older and younger men and women</li><li class=\"d1\">young men look to older men to see <b>what a Christian man looks like</b>; likewise women</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul knows <b>things women know about being a woman</b> that men don't, and vice versa</li><li class=\"d1\">\"weird misogynist\"? - Paul sees <b>female leadership</b> in churches, necessary function; early church way more than half women</li></ul>"},{"t":2034.76,"lv":3,"h":"2:6-8 - Titus and the young men","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:6-8</b> exhort young men to be <b>sober-minded</b> - \"not that young men are ever not sober-minded\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Titus to be a <b>pattern of good works</b>; integrity, reverence, incorruptibility, sound speech → opponent ashamed</li><li class=\"d0\">Titus' own role: <b>mentor to the young men</b></li><li class=\"d1\">doesn't say young men <b>and women</b> - older women help younger women (deaconesses in <b>1 Timothy</b>)</li><li class=\"d1\">because of <b>leadership role</b>, even more than other older men</li></ul>"},{"t":2103.81,"lv":2,"h":"2:9-10 - Slaves and masters","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:9-10</b> exhort <b>bondservants</b> to obey masters, not answer back, not pilfer, show <b>all good fidelity</b> → \"<b>adorn the doctrine of God our Savior</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"completely non-controversial\" - Paul: \"slaves obey your masters\"</li><li class=\"d1\">will say much more at <b>Philemon</b> intro</li><li class=\"d0\">Enslaved person conducts self as a <b>Christian</b> - same as free people</li><li class=\"d1\">goal: <b>witness of Christ to the one who enslaved them</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not just \"no slave rebellions\" - \"never work unless you're in Haiti, and that hasn't gone well either\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul doesn't think slavery is good - says as much</li><li class=\"d0\">Goal not to decide <b>who's oppressor, who's victim</b> and tell the oppressor to go to hell</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul wants Christ brought to <b>victim and oppressor</b></li><li class=\"d1\">involved in murder of people before Christ appeared to him; every one of us <b>harmed and harms</b>, <b>sinned against and sins</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no easy good person / bad person line - all created good but <b>dominated by sin</b>; God wants salvation for <b>every one</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2294.45,"lv":3,"h":"Wives, slaves, and Caesar","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Same as wives obeying husbands: Christian wife may be the means a <b>pagan husband</b> comes to Christ - even if he's a jerk, abusive</li><li class=\"d0\">Slaves, like women, came to Christianity <b>in droves</b> in the first centuries → means by which wealthy masters might come to Christ</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul in Rome to reach <b>Caesar</b> - \"essentially the <b>Antichrist</b>\", <b>Nero</b></li><li class=\"d1\">unlike fellow Judean <b>zealots</b> about to touch off rebellions that destroyed their province</li><li class=\"d1\">not overthrowing Caesar - <b>bringing Caesar to Christ</b>; happened 250 years later, Paul trying</li><li class=\"d0\">Hence slaves conduct themselves this way: hope of bringing the <b>household</b> to Christ</li></ul>"},{"t":2406.69,"lv":2,"h":"2:11-14 - Grace appeared; gospel and good works","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:11-14</b> grace of God that brings salvation has <b>appeared to all men</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christ manifesting himself - the theme from the beginning</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>teaching us</b> to deny ungodliness, live soberly, righteously, godly; looking for <b>blessed hope and glorious appearing</b> of our great God and Savior Jesus Christ</li><li class=\"d1\">who gave himself to <b>redeem</b> from lawless deed, purify <b>his own special people</b>, zealous for good works</li><li class=\"d1\">prefers KJV \"<b>peculiar</b> people\" - \"some of us are rather peculiar\"</li><li class=\"d0\">For Paul gospel = Christ gave himself, died, redeemed us from sin - <b>with a purpose</b>: fruit, <b>zealous for good works</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not two separate things - \"all one thing\"; theme throughout the epistle</li><li class=\"d1\">the one produces the other; <b>inseparable from salvation</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul <b>front-loads the way of life</b>: Christ appeared \"<b>teaching us</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">answers \"Paul never talks about Christ's <b>teaching</b>\" - summarized here</li><li class=\"d1\">Gospels: Christ teaching how to live → passion → giving himself → rising, sending us out to do good works</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul's summary = condensation of the <b>synoptic structure</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2606.17,"lv":3,"h":"2:15-3:2 - Speak with authority; subject to rulers","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:15</b> speak, exhort, rebuke with <b>all authority</b>; \"let no one despise you\"</li><li class=\"d1\">say this again and again, correct them</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:1-2</b> remind them to be <b>subject to rulers and authorities</b>, ready for every good work, <b>speak evil of no one</b>, peaceable, gentle, all humility</li><li class=\"d1\">same message as to the enslaved - Roman Empire is a <b>hierarchy</b>; unless you're Caesar, someone over you</li><li class=\"d1\">same reason: to help <b>bring them to Christ</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2687.06,"lv":2,"h":"3:3-7 - Not by works; Pharisaic narrative","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:3</b> \"we ourselves were also once <b>foolish, disobedient</b>, deceived, serving lusts, malice, envy, hateful\"</li><li class=\"d1\">all those people you don't want to be kind to or humble around - \"we were like that too\", just as bad, if not worse</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:4-5</b> when the <b>kindness and love of God</b> appeared (Christ again) - <b>not by works of righteousness</b>, but by <b>mercy</b>, through the <b>washing of regeneration</b> and renewing of the Holy Spirit</li><li class=\"d1\">nothing we did caused Christ to appear, through Paul's preaching or in the incarnation</li><li class=\"d0\">Why make that point? Paul a <b>Pharisee</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Pharisee MO: disobedience got us <b>exiled</b> → if all Judea obeys the law perfectly, <b>Messiah comes</b>, exile ends</li><li class=\"d1\">sinners had to be <b>rooted out</b> - hated tax collectors, wanted to stone them; \"keeping us in exile\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Pharisees <b>externalized</b> what should be internal: should root out sin in themselves and repent; put sin on others to remove them</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul directly counters it: Christ didn't appear because we got our act together</li><li class=\"d1\">like the <b>Exodus</b> - not because enslaved Israelites became good; <b>God's own timing</b>, he purified and set free</li></ul>"},{"t":2852.86,"lv":3,"h":"3:6-8 - Justified, heirs, maintain good works","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:6-7</b> Holy Spirit poured out abundantly; <b>justified by his grace</b> → <b>heirs</b> of the hope of eternal life</li><li class=\"d1\">justification like word processing: <b>left justified</b> = put in order, set straight</li><li class=\"d1\">by God's action put back in order, renewed, filled with Spirit</li><li class=\"d1\">heirs of the <b>promise to Abraham</b> of eternal life - sharing in God's life; true of even those miserable, defiled, sinful people through Christ</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:8</b> \"faithful saying\" - affirm constantly: those who believe <b>careful to maintain good works</b></li><li class=\"d1\">right after refuting Pharisaic narrative - lest someone misunderstand \"<b>Germany in 1517</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">those who received grace must <b>maintain good works</b> or might become out of order again, return to defilement and sin, <b>not experience the hope of eternal life</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"I don't think so\" to the other view</li></ul>"},{"t":2994.99,"lv":2,"h":"3:9-11 - Avoid disputes; three strikes","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:9</b> avoid foolish disputes, <b>genealogies</b>, strivings about the law - unprofitable and useless</li><li class=\"d1\">those sectarians: don't argue, don't wade into the \"muck of their detailed <b>internet debates</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:10-11</b> reject a <b>divisive man</b> after first and second admonition - warped, sinning, self-condemned</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>three strikes</b>: first, don't argue - warn; second warning; third → <b>out of the community</b> until behavior changes</li></ul>"},{"t":3057.16,"lv":2,"h":"3:12-15 - Closing and fruit","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:12-15</b> when I send <b>Artemas or Tychicus</b>, come to me at <b>Nicopolis</b> - decided to winter there</li><li class=\"d1\">send <b>Zenas the lawyer</b> and <b>Apollos</b> on their way, lacking nothing; \"let our people learn to maintain good works, to meet <b>urgent needs</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">all with me greet you; grace be with you all</li><li class=\"d0\">\"He's hoping\" - geography doesn't work; several Nicopolises (\"city of Victory\"), none fits when this was written</li><li class=\"d1\">presumably <b>earlier than 1 and 2 Timothy</b>, still thinks he may continue</li><li class=\"d1\">no direct comment on his passing as in <b>2 Timothy</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Stay in Crete until I send someone; then come to me - usual ending</li><li class=\"d1\">ends on \"<b>not unfruitful</b>\" - producing fruit = <b>part and parcel of the gospel</b></li></ul>"}]});
